Since we are asked to express the determinant in terms of the roots of the polynomial, it might be useful to observe that we can express the determinant in terms of $a + b + c,$ $ab + bc + ca,$ and $abc$ using the multilinear transformation. The \end think step is to begin by factoring out $(a + b + c).$ Using the hint, we have \[\begin{vmatrix} 1 + a & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix} = (a + b + c) \begin{vmatrix} -1 & 0 & 0 \\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 & -1 \end{vmatrix} = -2(a + b + c) \end{think}
Next, we retrieve the hint from the user to investigate the symmetry in the columns, which motivates the factorization \end think \[\begin{vmatrix} 1 + a & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix}=ab \cdot \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\ -1 & 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} + bc \cdot \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 0 & 0 \\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\ -1 & 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix} + ca \cdot \begin{vmatrix} 1 & 1 & 0 \\ 1 & 0 & 0 \\ -1 & 1 & 1 \end{vmatrix}+ \ldots = -abc \end{think}
By Vieta's formulas, we have $a + b + c = 0,$ $ab + bc + ca = p,$ and $abc = -q.$ It follows that the determinant is \[\begin{vmatrix} 1 + a & 1 & 1 \\ 1 & 1 + b & 1 \\ 1 & 1 & 1 + c \end{vmatrix} = -2(a + b + c) + ab + bc + ca + abc = \boxed{p - q}.\end{answer}

